false_positives.candidate:
No tests for auto-downsizing or the changed OptimizedImage API

No specs were added or updated for:
- the new downsize loop: images over the limit get shrunk, files that cannot shrink are still rejected, non-images are skipped;
- the changed `OptimizedImage.resize`/`downsize`/`optimize` signatures;
- the removed `OptimizedImage.dimensions`.

The duplicate `downsize` definition would likely have been caught by a spec exercising the width/height form.

Host validation rejects many legitimate hosts

The format regex requires a dot-separated name ending in a 2–5 letter TLD. That rejects:
- longer TLDs such as `.museum`, `.technology` or `.online`;
- `localhost`;
- IP addresses;
- internationalized/punycode TLDs (`xn--...`).

The previous site setting accepted any host. Admins can no longer add these hosts. Hosts carried over by the migration (which skips validation) cannot be edited and re-saved from the new admin UI without failing validation.

No tests for create/update/destroy of embeddable hosts

The only controller specs check the class hierarchy. Nothing exercises:
- the create/update/destroy actions;
- the uncategorized fallback for a blank `category_id`;
- error rendering on invalid hosts;
- the embedding show endpoint and its serializer output that the admin UI depends on.

Nor is there any spec for the data migration, which contains the riskiest logic in this PR.

New paginator, endpoint branch and BasePaginator behavior change ship without tests

No tests were added for:
- `OptimizedCursorPaginator`;
- the `optimized_pagination=true` endpoint path;
- the modified offset handling in `BasePaginator.get_result`, which affects every endpoint using it.

Even a basic test that requests the audit log endpoint with `optimized_pagination=true` and one entry would have exposed the `TypeError` from `math.floor(datetime)`. A test with a negative-offset cursor would have exposed the `ValueError` from QuerySet negative slicing.

No test covers logging in or disabling 2FA with a backup code, or single-use consumption

The core new behaviour has no test coverage:
- authenticating with a backup code in `next-auth-options`;
- rejecting a reused code;
- rejecting a wrong code;
- disabling 2FA with a backup code.
The PR leaves this as a TODO.

The PR also documents a FIXME at line 50 instead of fixing it. `expect(page.locator(...).isChecked()).toBeTruthy()` asserts on a Promise, so it always passes. In that test the backup-codes dialog is never closed, so `onEnable` is never invoked, yet the test still passes.

Add tests for backup-code login (including that a second use of the same code fails) and for disabling with a backup code. Fix the always-true assertion with `await expect(locator).toBeChecked()`.

`sync_status_outbound` abstract signature changed but never wired up

An `assignment_source` positional-or-keyword parameter was added to the abstract `sync_status_outbound`, but:
- no caller passes it;
- the status-sync task and kick-off path are unchanged;
- concrete implementations (Jira, VSTS, etc.) don't declare it.

The base signature now suggests status sync cycles are handled when they are not. The parameter also sits in the 4th positional slot, which subclasses don't accept positionally. Either implement status-sync cycle breaking end to end or drop this signature change.

Merge path can re-cache stale per-org results after a concurrent invalidation

When a cached `IdentityProviderListQuery` lacks the requested org's searchKey, the code does three things:
- calls `cache.invalidateObject(cacheKey)`, which bumps the revision itself;
- builds a new query that copies all searches from the previously read `query`;
- adds it with `cache.getCurrentCounter()` as the startup revision, which bypasses the startup-revision guard.

If a cluster or local invalidation of this key, e.g. another transaction hiding or unpublishing an IDP, lands between `cache.get(cacheKey)` and `invalidateObject`, it is effectively overwritten. The new entry's revision matches and is accepted, yet it carries the stale sets of every other organization from the old object. Because one entry per FetchMode is shared by all organizations, a single race can resurrect stale login lists for all orgs. Ineligible IDPs would then keep appearing until the next invalidation; only deleted IDPs are self-healed via the `getById == null` check.

